WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.668 --> 00:00:03.236
 Peter, Jordan,
congratulations.

00:00:03.303 --> 00:00:04.804
Gentlemen, you're moving forward
into the third and final round

00:00:04.871 --> 00:00:07.373
of our Forge of
Fear competition.

00:00:07.473 --> 00:00:09.308
Now, in this last round we're
sending you back to your home

00:00:09.409 --> 00:00:11.043
forges to work on
an iconic weapon

00:00:11.143 --> 00:00:13.212
from cinematic horror history.

00:00:13.279 --> 00:00:14.681
You're going to
be building this.

00:00:19.985 --> 00:00:21.121
The fireman's ax.

00:00:23.422 --> 00:00:25.090
 The fireman's ax is
the weapon of choice

00:00:25.190 --> 00:00:27.093
for some of the most
terrifying psychopaths

00:00:27.160 --> 00:00:28.795
in the horror genre.

00:00:28.895 --> 00:00:31.097
From Patrick Bateman
in "American Psycho"

00:00:31.197 --> 00:00:34.667
to Jack Torrence in Stanley
Kubrick's "The Shining,"

00:00:34.768 --> 00:00:36.335
ironically, this
notorious slasher

00:00:36.436 --> 00:00:39.038
was actually designed as
a robust life-saving tool

00:00:39.105 --> 00:00:42.508
used by brave firefighters
around the world.

00:00:42.575 --> 00:00:44.310
Featuring a strong,
stout ax blade

00:00:44.376 --> 00:00:47.513
on one side and a sharp,
spike-like pick on the other,

00:00:47.580 --> 00:00:49.515
it's designed to break
through doors and windows,

00:00:49.582 --> 00:00:52.285
pry through obstacles,
and clear debris to rescue

00:00:52.351 --> 00:00:54.420
people trapped in raging fires.

00:00:54.520 --> 00:00:57.490
 Gentlemen, you have four days
to build your firemens' axes.

00:00:57.590 --> 00:00:58.158
Good luck.

00:00:58.258 --> 00:00:59.292
We'll see you then.

00:00:59.392 --> 00:01:01.327 line:14%
[chuckling]

00:01:03.663 --> 00:01:05.565
 My name is Peter Farquhar.

00:01:05.665 --> 00:01:10.169
I'm 56 years old, and
I took up bladesmithing

00:01:10.236 --> 00:01:13.539
when I was 14 years old.

00:01:13.639 --> 00:01:15.641
My biggest concern
is that I'm going

00:01:15.742 --> 00:01:19.178
to do something really
stupid early on, because I

00:01:19.245 --> 00:01:20.679
just had a brain fart.

00:01:20.779 --> 00:01:23.182
I'm going to try and forge it
in one piece without a weld.

00:01:23.249 --> 00:01:24.984
It's time for me
to go to a narrower

00:01:25.051 --> 00:01:27.153
chisel that can take
more manageable bites out

00:01:27.220 --> 00:01:28.387
of the steel.

00:01:28.487 --> 00:01:29.722
I'm going to flood
it with grazing rods,

00:01:29.823 --> 00:01:31.591
so we'll have a
nice inlay effect.

00:01:31.691 --> 00:01:34.059
One side of the blade,
I have a Chinese dragon,

00:01:34.126 --> 00:01:35.528
in memory of my father.

00:01:35.595 --> 00:01:38.164
And on the other side, I
did a Norse-style dragon,

00:01:38.264 --> 00:01:39.398
in memory of my mother.

00:01:39.465 --> 00:01:40.834
Now I'm going to get
the handle ready.

00:01:44.903 --> 00:01:47.973
Well, my handle has developed
a crack right up the middle.

00:01:48.073 --> 00:01:49.175
That's worrisome.

00:01:49.242 --> 00:01:50.709
The judges could
give me a hard time,

00:01:50.776 --> 00:01:53.379
so the next thing I did was
I made a brass ferrule that

00:01:53.479 --> 00:01:55.014
will hold everything together.

00:01:55.114 --> 00:01:57.116
Structurally, I'm
not worried about it,

00:01:57.183 --> 00:01:59.252
but I am still upset
with myself that I

00:01:59.352 --> 00:02:00.519
made that little mistake.

00:02:00.586 --> 00:02:02.188
After that, I wanted to
do something interesting

00:02:02.288 --> 00:02:03.489
with the handle, too.

00:02:03.555 --> 00:02:05.290
I wound up making a
couple of branding irons

00:02:05.357 --> 00:02:08.427
and laying a scale pattern
on by burning it in.

00:02:08.527 --> 00:02:10.063
I don't know if I'm
going to win or not,

00:02:10.130 --> 00:02:11.798
but I know nobody is going
to forget this thing.

00:02:16.235 --> 00:02:17.403 line:14%
 My name's Jordan Jackson.

00:02:17.469 --> 00:02:19.538 line:8%
I'm from Rutherfordton,
North Carolina.

00:02:19.605 --> 00:02:22.141
I've been a bladesmith
for about eight years.

00:02:22.207 --> 00:02:23.809
I'm 32 years old.

00:02:23.876 --> 00:02:24.910
My son is 10 years old.

00:02:25.010 --> 00:02:27.013
He's made a few knives, himself.

00:02:27.079 --> 00:02:29.382
He gave me very
specific instructions

00:02:29.448 --> 00:02:32.184
to make sure that my
blades met parameters,

00:02:32.284 --> 00:02:34.053
and he told me not
to quench it too hot.

00:02:34.120 --> 00:02:38.490
I got my slot started there, so
I can drift out my handle hold.

00:02:38.557 --> 00:02:39.893
Going into the
heat treat, I'm not

00:02:39.959 --> 00:02:41.527
really concerned
about warps that much.

00:02:41.594 --> 00:02:43.729
My biggest concern is
any of these forge welds

00:02:43.796 --> 00:02:44.797
wanting to pop loose.

00:02:44.897 --> 00:02:46.365
It's a very delicate process.

00:02:46.465 --> 00:02:48.567
If the welds aren't
successful, I'm

00:02:48.634 --> 00:02:50.570
gonna have to go all the
way back to square one,

00:02:50.637 --> 00:02:52.071
and start all over.

00:02:52.171 --> 00:02:55.040
I go with the spot
first, and then I flip

00:02:55.140 --> 00:02:56.576
it over and quench the blade.

00:02:59.812 --> 00:03:00.980
Oh, yeah.

00:03:01.080 --> 00:03:02.614
File skates and it's
absolutely beautiful.

00:03:02.715 --> 00:03:04.650
Wanted to add a little bit
of design to the handle,

00:03:04.750 --> 00:03:07.352
so I did some tally
marks for a body count.

00:03:07.452 --> 00:03:08.921
I think it's going to look cool.

00:03:08.988 --> 00:03:11.391
So I'm getting ready to put
the ax head onto the handle.

00:03:11.457 --> 00:03:15.094
I slide it on there, tap it
under the handle to make sure

00:03:15.161 --> 00:03:16.628
that everything's set good.

00:03:16.695 --> 00:03:17.930
Cool.

00:03:17.997 --> 00:03:19.732
So now I want to go back on
that hickory handle and sort

00:03:19.799 --> 00:03:22.802
of char it a little bit, to make
it look like it's a really old,

00:03:22.902 --> 00:03:24.269
found murder weapon.

00:03:24.369 --> 00:03:26.572
You don't see an ax
murderer walking around

00:03:26.638 --> 00:03:28.341
with a brand new ax
that they just picked

00:03:28.441 --> 00:03:31.477
up at the hardware store.

00:03:31.578 --> 00:03:32.544
Look at that.

00:03:32.611 --> 00:03:34.380
I'm really happy with
how that came out.

00:03:34.447 --> 00:03:38.017
 Bladesmiths, welcome
to the kill test.

00:03:38.117 --> 00:03:39.485
Today, I'm going
to take your weapon

00:03:39.552 --> 00:03:43.022
and fend it against
this horned demon.

00:03:43.089 --> 00:03:44.056
Peter, you're first.

00:03:44.122 --> 00:03:45.624
Shall be free it of its soul?

00:03:45.691 --> 00:03:46.492
 Oh, please.

00:03:46.559 --> 00:03:47.025
 All right.

00:03:47.092 --> 00:03:47.894
Let's do it.

00:04:12.451 --> 00:04:15.354
All right, Peter, let's
talk about your ax here.

00:04:15.420 --> 00:04:17.056
The ax handle you have
here is wide enough

00:04:17.123 --> 00:04:20.626
to where I can get a very good
grip in wielding this weapon.

00:04:20.693 --> 00:04:23.229
Your ax head is
very sharp, and more

00:04:23.295 --> 00:04:27.065
importantly, sir, it will kill.

00:04:27.132 --> 00:04:28.334
 Thank you.

00:04:28.401 --> 00:04:30.102
 All right, Jordan,
you're up next.

00:04:30.169 --> 00:04:31.537
Are you ready for this?
- Oh, yeah.

00:04:31.638 --> 00:04:32.538
Let's do this.

00:04:56.095 --> 00:04:59.298
 All right, George, let's
talk about your fireman's ax.

00:04:59.365 --> 00:05:03.068
I love the feel, and the
shape of your handle.

00:05:03.169 --> 00:05:05.504
The head you have
here is heavier,

00:05:05.604 --> 00:05:10.309
so it really penetrates
deep into this demon dummy.

00:05:10.375 --> 00:05:13.512
Your edge took no
damage whatsoever,

00:05:13.579 --> 00:05:16.615
and I think now we can add
another notch to your handle.

00:05:16.682 --> 00:05:21.988
Overall, sir, your
fireman's ax, it will kill.

00:05:22.055 --> 00:05:22.888
 Thanks, Doug.

00:05:22.955 --> 00:05:23.589
That's what I needed.

00:05:26.158 --> 00:05:26.926
 Hi, guys.

00:05:29.996 --> 00:05:33.132
Well, bladesmiths, welcome
to our strength test.

00:05:33.232 --> 00:05:37.102
Our horror door chop.

00:05:37.203 --> 00:05:38.871
Now, what the axes
do to the door

00:05:38.938 --> 00:05:41.807
is secondary to what the
door does to your axes,

00:05:41.874 --> 00:05:44.409
but regardless, it's going
to be a hell of a lot of fun.

00:05:44.476 --> 00:05:46.078
Now, Peter, you ready to go?

00:05:46.178 --> 00:05:46.878
 I'm ready.
Let's go.

00:05:46.945 --> 00:05:47.713
 All right.

00:05:47.780 --> 00:05:48.548
Let's do it.

00:05:56.122 --> 00:05:57.724
[grunting]

00:06:13.506 --> 00:06:14.774
Becky, I'm home.

00:06:18.243 --> 00:06:19.512
All right, Peter, nice job.

00:06:19.612 --> 00:06:20.646
Everything's still tight.

00:06:23.983 --> 00:06:28.754
You got a couple little small
splits there from wedging it.

00:06:28.821 --> 00:06:33.392
Your spike is still perfectly
sharp, and same with your edge.

00:06:33.492 --> 00:06:35.160
Still razor sharp.

00:06:35.227 --> 00:06:35.928
Good job.

00:06:35.995 --> 00:06:37.262
 Thank you much.

00:06:37.362 --> 00:06:38.831
 All right, Jordan, you're up.

00:06:38.898 --> 00:06:41.100
You ready to give this pup
of a door its medicine?

00:06:41.200 --> 00:06:41.967
 Oh, yeah.

00:06:42.034 --> 00:06:42.802
 Let's do it.

00:06:51.377 --> 00:06:53.513
[grunting]

00:07:02.687 --> 00:07:03.556
 All right, Jordan, nice job.

00:07:03.656 --> 00:07:06.959
This is a much heavier ax.

00:07:07.026 --> 00:07:08.861
Wider handle.

00:07:08.928 --> 00:07:10.396
Edge still razor sharp.

00:07:10.496 --> 00:07:12.831
Nothing loosened up.

00:07:12.932 --> 00:07:13.733
Real nice job.

00:07:13.833 --> 00:07:14.266
 Awesomme.

00:07:14.333 --> 00:07:16.936
Thanks.

00:07:17.036 --> 00:07:21.140
 Bladesmiths, welcome
to our sharpness test--

00:07:21.207 --> 00:07:24.276
the arm chop.

00:07:24.343 --> 00:07:25.377
Peter, you're up first.

00:07:25.444 --> 00:07:26.244
Are you ready, sir?

00:07:26.311 --> 00:07:27.245
 I am ready.

00:07:27.312 --> 00:07:28.213
 All right.

00:07:28.313 --> 00:07:29.182
Let's have some hand chops.

00:07:45.898 --> 00:07:47.099
All right, Peter.

00:07:47.199 --> 00:07:51.270
Sharp edges, and these are
all very clean cuts, sir.

00:07:51.370 --> 00:07:54.272
Overall, your
weapon-- it will cut.

00:07:54.373 --> 00:07:55.808
 Thanks, Doug.

00:07:55.908 --> 00:07:57.409
 All right, Jordan, your turn.
Sir, are you ready?

00:07:57.476 --> 00:07:59.011
- Oh, yeah.
- All right, let's do this.

00:08:18.063 --> 00:08:19.765
All right, Jordan, even
though it's heavier,

00:08:19.832 --> 00:08:23.603
it is the weight that's expected
of a firemen's ax, because just

00:08:23.703 --> 00:08:26.939
let the weight fall into this,
and it cut cleanly on the arm.

00:08:27.006 --> 00:08:31.477
Overall, sir, your
weapon, it will cut.

00:08:31.544 --> 00:08:32.512
 Thanks, Doug.

00:08:36.014 --> 00:08:37.582
 Well, gentlemen,
we tried to scare you

00:08:37.682 --> 00:08:39.418
guys away in this Forge
of Fear competition,

00:08:39.519 --> 00:08:41.754
but you both stood up and
showed us you guys have

00:08:41.820 --> 00:08:43.356
the skills it takes to be here.

00:08:43.422 --> 00:08:44.924
But in this competition,
only one of you guys

00:08:45.024 --> 00:08:46.725
will be going home with the
title of "Forged in Fire"

00:08:46.792 --> 00:08:49.261
champion, and a
check for $10,000.

00:08:49.361 --> 00:08:50.595
The judges have discussed it.

00:08:50.695 --> 00:08:52.965
It took a while, but
they made a decision.

00:08:53.032 --> 00:08:55.067
Today's "Forged in
Fire" champion is--

00:08:58.303 --> 00:08:59.071
Jordan.

00:08:59.138 --> 00:09:00.439
Congratulations.

00:09:00.506 --> 00:09:02.041
Now, Peter, you did
a phenomenal job,

00:09:02.141 --> 00:09:04.376
but unfortunately, your blade is
not going to end up on the wall

00:09:04.443 --> 00:09:05.511
today.

00:09:05.578 --> 00:09:06.512
And Jay is going
to tell you why.

00:09:06.612 --> 00:09:08.047
 Peter, you did
a fantastic job,

00:09:08.113 --> 00:09:10.449
and you brought us an
ax that I would take

00:09:10.516 --> 00:09:12.084
home and use in a heartbeat.

00:09:12.151 --> 00:09:13.718
But we have a handle issue.

00:09:13.819 --> 00:09:16.922
We do have a crack that
goes all the way through,

00:09:16.989 --> 00:09:19.859
and Jordan's design
is much closer

00:09:19.926 --> 00:09:22.161
to the example of the
traditional fireman's ax.

00:09:22.228 --> 00:09:24.763
And that's why we didn't
choose your weapon today.

00:09:24.863 --> 00:09:27.132
 Now, Peter, you crushed it
in the first couple of rounds

00:09:27.199 --> 00:09:29.067
of this competition, and
you did phenomenally well

00:09:29.134 --> 00:09:31.270
in the last round, but
unfortunately, your time

00:09:31.336 --> 00:09:32.438
in this competition has ended.

00:09:32.504 --> 00:09:33.472
I'm gonna have to
ask you to please

00:09:33.539 --> 00:09:35.374
step off the forge floor.

00:09:35.441 --> 00:09:36.475
- Thank you, gentlemen.
- Thank you.

00:09:36.541 --> 00:09:37.375
 Great job, Peter.

00:09:37.476 --> 00:09:39.445
You did great.

00:09:39.512 --> 00:09:41.179
 If I had another piece
of wood lying around,

00:09:41.279 --> 00:09:44.616 line:8%
I would have junked the handle
and started over right then.

00:09:44.683 --> 00:09:47.153
But I respect the
decision they made.

00:09:47.219 --> 00:09:48.588
 Jordan, congratulations, man.

00:09:48.654 --> 00:09:50.088
That means you are the
champion of "Forged in Fire,"

00:09:50.188 --> 00:09:52.892
and you just won yourself
a check for $10,000.

00:09:52.959 --> 00:09:54.894
Congratulations.

00:09:54.961 --> 00:09:57.396
 I'm super proud of what
I was able to accomplish,

00:09:57.496 --> 00:09:59.264
and you know, I just
wanted to make my family

00:09:59.331 --> 00:10:00.665
and my community proud.

00:10:00.732 --> 00:10:02.701
 That thing is a beast.

00:10:02.801 --> 00:10:05.137
 My name is Jordan Jackson,
and I'm the "Forged in Fire"

00:10:05.237 --> 00:10:06.872
champion.

